37:The `awww` daemon paints the wallpaper, so `swaybg` is never used, but the package remains installed. It cannot be removed through dnf: it is a hard dependency of `sway-config-fedora`, which owns the SDDM session entry point (`/usr/bin/start-sway`, `sway.desktop`), the `layered-include` helper that the Sway config includes, and the `sway/config.d` fragments, so `dnf remove swaybg` cascades into removing `sway-config-fedora`, `sway-systemd`, `sddm`, `sddm-wayland-sway` and `swayfx`, leaving no way to log in graphically.
43:The ASUS TUF Gaming A15 FA506II is a hybrid laptop: the internal panel is wired to the AMD Renoir iGPU, and the NVIDIA GTX 1650 Ti is a discrete GPU that sits idle. The proprietary NVIDIA 615 driver is installed from RPM Fusion (`akmod-nvidia-615.78.08`, `xorg-x11-drv-nvidia-615.78.08` and friends) and replaces `nouveau`, which is blacklisted in `/etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-nouveau.conf`. `/etc/modprobe.d/nvidia-drm.conf` enables `nvidia_drm modeset=1`.
45:RPM Fusion's `99-nvidia-dracut.conf` keeps the NVIDIA modules out of the ramdisk so that kernel updates do not need a ramdisk rebuild. That is wrong once `nouveau` is blacklisted, because the proprietary driver has to claim the device early. `~/.local/bin/sway-nvidia-initramfs` rebuilds the ramdisk with `--add-drivers "nvidia nvidia-modeset nvidia-drm nvidia-uvm"`; run it after a kernel update.
